# SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 The DocUp contributors
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# SPEC 9.4: level 9 in the namespaces where a type error is a security bug -
# request resolution, authorisation, artifact handling and configuration
# parsing. Run alongside the repo-wide level 8, not instead of it.

includes:
    - vendor/larastan/larastan/extension.neon

parameters:
    level: 9

    paths:
        - app/Serving
        - app/Auth
        - app/Artifacts
        - app/Config
        # Phase 05. SPEC 9.4 names serving, authentication, artifact and
        # configuration; source connection is added because a type error in
        # the SSRF deny list or the credential envelope is a security bug by
        # the same argument. Raising a gate is permitted; lowering one is not.
        - app/Egress
        - app/Source
        - app/Credentials
        - app/Logging
        # Phase 06. The build pipeline renders the argv a container runtime
        # executes and carries the one credential that reaches a sandbox,
        # so SPEC 9.4's argument applies with full force: a type error here
        # is a security bug rather than a crash.
        #
        # 🛑 app/Console/Commands is deliberately NOT here. `RunnerCommand`
        # holds the socket, but all of its logic is in app/Builds - the
        # command is a claim loop. Adding the namespace would pull in
        # `doctor` and `install`, which predate this bar by five phases, and
        # raising a gate for code a phase did not touch is how a phase ends
        # up either ignoring findings or growing a second scope.
        - app/Builds
        # Phase 11, and §9.4's argument applies here more plainly than
        # anywhere it has yet been applied. `app/Webhooks` is the
        # application's ONLY unauthenticated write path: a type error in a
        # signature comparison, a replay check or a provider payload is a
        # security bug by construction, not a crash. `app/Automation` joins
        # it because §8.6.15's `delete_version` is the one operation in
        # DocUp that destroys reader-visible URLs.
        - app/Webhooks
        - app/Automation
        # Phase 13. §8.7.7 calls an unchecked component mount "a
        # declassification primitive in a product whose headline feature is
        # per-project authentication", which is §9.4's criterion stated by
        # the requirement itself: a type error in the ceiling comparison is
        # a security bug, not a crash.
        - app/Composition
        # Phase 16. §8.12.8 says composition "MUST NEVER widen access,
        # **without exception**", and `App\Collections\MountExposure` is the
        # write-time half of that refusal — §9.4's criterion again, stated by
        # the requirement: a type error in the narrowing comparison publishes
        # a private project at a collection URL.
        - app/Collections
        # Phase 18. §8.10.4 puts proof of control before "any byte served for
        # a custom hostname" and §8.10.5's generated column is what stops a
        # squatter blocking the owner — §9.4's criterion stated by the
        # requirement: a type error here serves one tenant's readers another
        # tenant's site.
        - app/Domains
        # Phase 19. §8.10.11's permission endpoint is the only thing between
        # an unauthenticated TLS handshake and a certificate authority, and
        # §8.10.14's store hands private keys to the edge — §9.4's criterion
        # stated twice over: a type error that made a denial look like a
        # grant spends the installation's issuance budget, and one in the
        # store's AAD serves a hostname a key that is not its own.
        - app/Tls
        # Phase 20. §8.11.2 says a redirect target must not become "an open
        # redirect or a traffic-exfiltration primitive", and `RedirectTarget`
        # is the only thing standing between an operator's typo and one —
        # §9.4's criterion stated by the requirement. §8.11.4's regex
        # containment is in the same namespace and has the same character: a
        # type error in the backtracking guard is a serving-path denial of
        # service, not a crash.
        - app/Redirects
        # And §8.11.6's generated files, which tell a crawler which of N
        # domains is canonical and which versions exist. A type error that
        # put a hidden version in a sitemap would publish something §8.6
        # deliberately unadvertised.
        - app/Seo
        # Phase 21. §9.4's criterion needs no restating here: §12.3 makes one
        # function the sole decider of every reader request, so a type error in
        # it is the disclosure the whole of §8.12 exists to prevent. It is also
        # the namespace where level 9's value is highest, because the code is
        # pure — there is no I/O for a type error to hide behind, and every
        # finding is a finding about the logic.
        - app/Access
        # Phase 22. §8.12.11's grant is the one credential in the product that
        # mints read access to every private project, and §8.12.12 forbids the
        # minting key reaching any edge component — so a type error in the
        # verifier's claim handling, the audience comparison or the epoch
        # comparison is the disclosure §8.12 exists to prevent, not a crash.
        # §8.12.10's handoff is in the same namespace and has the same
        # character: a type error in the single-use latch mints two sessions
        # from one code, and one in the return path is an open redirect.
        - app/Readers
        - app/Support
        - app/Concerns
        - app/Models
        - app/Http/Middleware

    excludePaths:
        analyseAndScan:
            - vendor

